  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday night through next Friday)\nIssued at 155 PM CDT Fri Aug 14 2026\n\nOur area will have a continuation of hot and dry conditions Sunday \nthrough Wednesday with mostly clear skies, with the prevailing \ninfluence from a large and expansive upper level ridge. The center \nof this ridge will shift to the west-northwest and be over the \nArkLaTex Monday, North Texas on Tuesday, and over the southwestern \nCONUS in the middle to late week time frame. While little change is \nexpected with daily low temperatures, the 850mb thermal ridge is \nprogged to strengthen over our area in the early to middle parts of \nnext week. This favors an uptick in the daily highs, with most of \nour area at or above 100 degrees, and a larger part of the Big \nCountry and some of the Concho Valley pushing Heat Advisory \ncriteria. South winds will continue each day, but expect wind speeds \nshould to be a little lower Sunday through Tuesday as the surface \npressure gradient weakens to some extent. Increased south winds are \nindicated Wednesday. \n\nOur area may see an interruption to this pattern that may last 2-3 \ndays, late in the week. With the westward shift in the position of \nthe upper ridge, a shortwave upper trough is progged to move east-\nsoutheast across the central Plains in the Wednesday to Thursday \ntime frame. This could allow an associated weak cold front to move \nsouth into our area Thursday into Friday. Isolated to scattered \nshowers and thunderstorms will be possible with the front, but there \nis uncertainty at this time in how far south the front would \nprogress, and the timing. With increased cloud cover, temperatures \nwould be a little cooler as well. Something to monitor with \nsubsequent model data and model trends.",
